.TH BUBELET 8 "October 2026" "Bubernetes" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
bubelet \- the Bubernetes node daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bubelet
.RB [ \-\-name
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-bind
.IR IP ]
.RB [ \-\-port
.IR PORT ]
.RB [ \-\-manifests
.IR DIR ]
.RB [ \-\-seed
.IR IP:PORT "]..."
.RB [ \-\-certs
.IR DIR ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bubelet
is the whole of a Bubernetes node. Every machine in the cluster runs one, and
nothing else: it maintains membership, replicates the desired state, decides
which workloads this node should run, and runs them. There are no other
components. See
.BR bubernetes (7)
for the model.
.PP
On start it reads its manifest directory, contacts any
.B \-\-seed
nodes to join the cluster, and from then on keeps the processes it is
responsible for running: starting missing replicas, stopping surplus ones,
restarting ones that exit, and fetching from a peer any workload image it does
not yet hold.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-name " NAME"
The node's identity in the cluster. Defaults to the hostname. Must be 1..63
characters of
.BR [A-Za-z0-9._-] .
A name must be unique and should be stable across restarts; a new machine that
starts with an existing node's name
.I is
that node as far as the cluster is concerned.
.TP
.BI \-\-bind " IP"
The IPv4 address to listen on. This is also the address announced to peers as
the way to reach this node, so it must be routable from the other nodes.
Default
.BR 127.0.0.1 ,
which is only useful for a single-machine cluster.
.TP
.BI \-\-port " PORT"
One port, used for both the UDP membership protocol and the TCP wire. Default
.BR 7700 .
.TP
.BI \-\-manifests " DIR"
The manifest directory, the node's only storage. Created if missing. Default
.IR /etc/bubernetes/manifests .
.TP
.BI \-\-seed " IP:PORT"
A node already in the cluster, to join through. May be repeated. The first node
of a cluster needs none. Joining does not require TLS.
.TP
.BI \-\-certs " DIR"
Enable mutual TLS on the TCP wire using
.IR DIR/ca.pem ,
.I DIR/node.pem
and
.IR DIR/node.key .
Only available when built with
.BR "make TLS=1" ;
ignored otherwise.
.SH WORKLOAD ENVIRONMENT
A replica is started with
.I argv[0]
set to the resource name followed by the manifest's
.BR argv ,
and the environment
.TP
.B BUBE_NAME
the resource name
.TP
.B BUBE_NODE
this node's name
.TP
.B BUBE_REPLICA
the replica's index on this node
.TP
.B PATH
.I /usr/bin:/bin
.PP
Its standard output and error are those of
.BR bubelet .
It is sent
.B SIGTERM
when it should stop and
.B SIGKILL
if it has not exited five seconds later, and it receives
.B SIGTERM
automatically if
.B bubelet
itself dies. A replica that exits within ten seconds of starting is treated as
a crash and its restarts back off exponentially (2s, 4s, ... 60s) while the
resource shows
.BR Unhealthy ;
a daemon that forks and leaves triggers exactly this (see
.BR bubernetes (7)).
.SH SIGNALS
.B SIGTERM
and
.B SIGINT
stop the daemon: every workload it started is sent
.B SIGTERM
and reaped before it exits.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/bubernetes/manifests/
edit files, state files,
.IR .scan ,
.IR .lamport ;
see
.BR bubernetes (7).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on a clean stop, 1 if it could not bind its sockets or set up TLS, 2 on a
usage error.
.SH EXAMPLES
The first node of a cluster, listening on its LAN address:
.PP
.nf
# bubelet --bind 10.0.0.7
.fi
.PP
A second machine joining it:
.PP
.nf
# bubelet --bind 10.0.0.161 --seed 10.0.0.7:7700
.fi
.PP
Both with mutual TLS, after
.B examples/gen-certs.sh certs node-a node-b
(the certificate names must match
.BR \-\-name ):
.PP
.nf
# bubelet --name node-a --bind 10.0.0.7   --certs certs/node-a
# bubelet --name node-b --bind 10.0.0.161 --certs certs/node-b --seed 10.0.0.7:7700
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bubernetes (7),
.BR bubectl (1)
